# 9. ASSESSMENT MODES

P38 must expose which level of analysis is actually supported by the available inputs and calculation engine.

## Mode A — Geometric Daylight Potential

Evaluate the geometry and obvious constraints influencing daylight potential.

Possible outputs include:

- Window-to-floor-area ratio (where both areas are valid).
- Window orientation.
- Available glazed area.
- Room depth relative to window and room dimensions.
- Potential external obstruction.
- Presence or absence of supported shading.
- Geometry-derived daylight access indicators.
- Missing input details.
- Confirmed or unconfirmed natural-light requirements.

These values describe geometry and potential. They are not equivalent to measured illuminance, daylight factor or annual daylight autonomy.

Do not claim that a specific window-to-floor ratio proves compliance or sufficient daylight.

## Mode B — Solar Position and Direct-Sun Screening

Calculate or screen solar position and the relationship between solar direction, building orientation, window geometry and available shading.

A defensible calculation may require:

- Latitude and longitude.
- Date.
- Local time or a supported time-series input.
- Time-zone and daylight-saving handling where relevant.
- North-orientation convention.
- Window and building orientation.
- Shading geometry.
- Any required terrain or external obstruction information.

Do not infer latitude, longitude or precise local solar conditions from a project name or city string.

If the necessary location or time inputs are unavailable, allow a user-defined hypothetical scenario and label it appropriately.

Identify likely periods or directions of direct-sun penetration only when the implemented solar and shadow model supports that output.

Do not fabricate a live sun position or shadow geometry.

## Mode C — Simplified Daylight Calculation

Offer a numerical calculation only when the required input properties and a defensible method are available.

The implementation must document:

- The actual model.
- Assumptions.
- Required inputs.
- Units.
- Sky or environmental boundary conditions.
- Supported geometry.
- Limitations.
- Independent expected-value tests.

If a simplified daylight-factor model is implemented, it must use a physically coherent daylight method and explicit sky conditions. Do not invent a daylight factor from window area or room depth alone.

Do not claim that a simplified calculation is an EN 17037-compliant assessment unless the applicable method and required conditions have genuinely been implemented and verified.

## Mode D — Climate-Based Annual Daylight Modelling

Provide spatial Daylight Autonomy (sDA), Annual Sunlight Exposure (ASE), Daylight Autonomy (DA), Useful Daylight Illuminance (UDI), or other annual metrics only if the actual engine implements the necessary validated method and the input data supports it.

Annual analysis may require:

- Suitable hourly weather or daylight-climate data.
- Exact location and time conventions.
- Relevant sky and solar conditions.
- Valid room and glazing geometry.
- Optical properties.
- Surface reflectance.
- Analysis grid.
- Shading control assumptions.
- Required occupancy or analysis schedules.
- A reproducible simulation configuration.

Do not label an annual metric as calculated if the implementation merely uses static geometry, one sun angle or an arbitrary daylight score.

If no genuine climate-based daylight engine is available, show annual simulation as unavailable and provide Modes A/B plus any genuinely supported Mode C calculation.

Do not falsely claim that a Three.js rendering pass is a Radiance-quality or otherwise validated lighting simulation.

## Mode E — Visual Comfort, Glare and View Out

Assess glare and view-out opportunities only to the level supported by the model and data.

Where available, consider:

- Window position and visible area.
- Direct-sun penetration.
- Field of view.
- Bright-sky or bright-window exposure.
- Shading and blind configurations.
- Potentially obstructed views.
- Room use and user-selected visual priorities.

Distinguish a qualitative glare concern from a validated glare metric.

Do not fabricate Daylight Glare Probability (DGP), luminance maps or eye-level glare predictions from a simple geometric score.

If an accurate glare calculation requires luminance distribution or a validated simulation that the engine does not provide, show a qualitative risk assessment with its limitations.

## Mode F — Scenario Comparison

Compare supported daylight and shading configurations while keeping the underlying room and environmental assumptions consistent when required.

Example scenarios:

- Existing window configuration.
- Alternative window size or position.
- Different glazing VT.
- Exterior overhang versus no overhang.
- Different blind or shading states.
- Changed room surface reflectance assumptions.
- Different hypothetical sun positions or supported sky conditions.
- Alternative design options supplied through the actual P31 contract.

For each comparison, show which inputs changed and whether the resulting outputs remain comparable.

Do not compare a measured value in one scenario with an illustrative prediction in another as if the evidence were equivalent.

# 10. PHYSICAL MODEL AND NUMERICAL GUARDRAILS

All numerical outputs must be produced through testable service functions.

Use explicit units, validate inputs, preserve internal precision and make calculations deterministic for the same input set and method version.

Reject or qualify invalid, missing or unsupported data.

Avoid hidden default optical properties and fallback coordinates.

If the feature uses a simplified geometric proxy, label it as a proxy.

Do not turn an approximate daylight-access indicator into a lux value or annual-performance result.

Do not add a universal correction factor to force calculated values to match a desired score.

For each implemented calculation, provide independent numerical test fixtures and documented tolerances.

A result should identify:

- Its method.
- Its input set.
- Its environmental assumptions.
- Its data provenance.
- Its supported range or geometry.
- Its principal limitations.
- The conditions that would make the result stale.

If a result cannot be calculated meaningfully, return an insufficient-evidence state and explain which missing inputs are necessary.

# 11. VISUAL COMFORT AND GLARE

Visual comfort is broader than whether the room looks bright.

Support structured screening for:

- Potential excessive direct sunlight.
- Bright-window exposure.
- Possible glare from low-angle sunlight.
- Potentially inadequate daylight access.
- Shading strategy conflicts.
- View-out obstruction.
- Room-use requirements that may affect visual priorities.
- Uncertain results caused by missing optical or environmental data.

Do not conflate high daylight with good visual comfort.

Do not automatically classify every east- or west-facing window as uncomfortable without considering actual geometry, time, shading and applicable assumptions.

Do not treat a rendered bloom, brightness or emissive material as a scientific glare calculation.

If the available model only supports qualitative screening, explain what further input or specialist analysis would be necessary to calculate a recognised glare metric.

# 12. SHADING AND SOLAR-CONTROL SCENARIOS

Allow users to examine supported alternatives involving:

- External overhangs.
- Side fins.
- Louvers.
- Interior blinds.
- Exterior shading devices.
- Changed shading dimensions.
- Changed window orientation or size.
- Different shading-state assumptions.

Do not assume all shading devices have the same optical behaviour.

Do not assume a blind blocks direct sunlight completely or that it transmits a known percentage without relevant optical data.

Where supported, produce geometric sun/shadow results under a documented solar position. Otherwise, provide a clearly labelled conceptual comparison.

Explain the trade-off between reducing direct-sun exposure and reducing daylight availability.

Do not calculate energy savings, cooling demand or operational carbon as a P38 result unless the necessary validated model exists. Those claims must not be generated from the visual comparison alone.

# 13. DAYLIGHT CRITERIA AND REGULATORY STATUS

Do not hard-code one universal target for daylight factor, sDA, ASE, lux, sunlight duration or glare across all room types and jurisdictions.

Provide a configurable criteria model where appropriate.

A configured criterion should identify, as applicable:

- Room or activity type.
- Metric and units.
- Target or range.
- Source.
- Jurisdiction and applicable standard.
- Edition or version.
- Applicability conditions.
- Whether it is advisory, project-defined or legally required.
- Verification status.

Keep user-defined preferences separate from authoritative requirements.

A reference value is not automatically a statutory requirement.

A result that satisfies one daylight metric does not automatically satisfy every aspect of daylight provision, glare protection, view out or sunlight exposure.

Do not claim compliance with EN 17037, ANSI/IES LM-83, or a local building code merely because a displayed threshold is met.

Verify current public metadata and scope before documenting a standard as current. Use standards content only where licensing and access conditions permit.

