# 19. TECHNICAL REFERENCES AND RESEARCH RULES

Verify relevant public source information and the applicable edition before documenting a method as current.

Useful starting references:

- **EN 17037:2018+A1:2021 — Daylight in buildings**, current listing and scope information from BSI: https://landingpage.bsigroup.com/LandingPage/Undated?UPI=000000000030342286
- **ANSI/IES LM-83-23 — Spatial Daylight Autonomy (sDA) and Annual Sunlight Exposure (ASE)**, official IES product information: https://store.ies.org/product/approved-method-ies-spatial-daylight-autonomy-sda-and-annual-sunlight-exposure-ase/
- **ISO 15469:2004 — Spatial distribution of daylight — CIE standard general sky**, official ISO record: https://www.iso.org/standard/38608.html
- **ISO/CIE 15469**, new edition published in 2026 and described by ISO as under publication in its current record: https://www.iso.org/standard/90136.html

These sources serve different purposes.

EN 17037 addresses daylight provision, view out, sunlight exposure and glare protection principles. ANSI/IES LM-83-23 defines annual sDA and ASE methods that require suitable climate-based calculations, including hourly weather inputs. ISO/CIE 15469 concerns standardised sky luminance distributions. None of these sources by itself proves that P38 implements the relevant method.

Do not reproduce copyrighted standards text, tables or protected worked examples. Do not upload licensed publications into AI tools unless the applicable licence explicitly permits it.

Use public metadata, permitted source descriptions and independently documented original calculations. Cite source titles, editions and scope without implying that an unimplemented standard has been applied.

